======================================================================================================================
g04d -- assessing the two proposed cluster doors
======================================================================================================================

  E1  Door 1.  Re-deriving the clock's acceleration from the same expansion used in g03t, once with a
      general lapse and once with a PROJECTABLE lapse N = N(t) (so the lapse perturbation carries no x).
      general lapse    : J_x = Derivative(Psi(t, x), x) - Derivative(T(t, x), t, x)
      projectable lapse: J_x = -Derivative(T(t, x), t, x)
  [PASS] E1 [door 1] with a general lapse the clock's linear acceleration is the gradient of (Psi - T-dot), the object that carries the MOND source in this action; with a projectable lapse only the T-dot piece survives   (general J_x = Derivative(Psi(t, x), x) - Derivative(T(t, x), t, x); projectable J_x = -Derivative(T(t, x), t, x))

  E2  the static limit is what carries galaxy phenomenology: there the clock is unperturbed in time, T-dot = 0.
      projectable lapse, static: J_x = 0
  [PASS] E2 [door 1 FAILS] with a projectable lapse the clock's acceleration vanishes identically in the static limit, so the coupling 2(2-K_B) J.dphi has no source and this action produces NO MOND force at all: projectability buys the cold dust by removing the modification it was meant to complete   (J_x = 0 exactly: the static MOND source is switched off, so Door 1 as posed replaces the cluster problem with the loss of galaxy phenomenology)

  E3  Door 2.  The cluster potential from the observed hydrostatic profiles, against the required a0 boost.
       r [kpc]    |Phi|/c^2   a0_req/a0
            40    1.015e-04       24.12
            50    1.005e-04       22.93
            75    9.830e-05       20.55
           100    9.616e-05       19.44
           150    9.216e-05       20.05
           200    8.854e-05       18.65
           300    8.233e-05       14.97
           420    7.644e-05       12.74
           750    6.521e-05        8.39
      d log|Phi|/d log r = -0.143;  d log(a0_req)/d log r = -0.317
      one-parameter fit of F(u) = 1 + beta u^2/(1+u) with chi_0 = 2e-06: best beta = 0.376, rms 0.080 dex
      F(fit) :   19.7   19.5   19.1   18.7   18.0   17.3   16.1   15.0   12.9
      required:   24.1   22.9   20.6   19.4   20.0   18.6   15.0   12.7    8.4
      this is a FAIR fit and is reported as such: the amplitude is reproduced, and the residual is at the ends, where
      the required boost falls by 2.9x across 40-750 kpc while the potential-driven F falls by only 1.5x.
  [PASS] E3 [door 2, reported and NOT an objection] one parameter reproduces the required a0 boost to under 0.1 dex rms, so the radial profile is not what defeats this proposal; the residual is a shape mismatch at the ends, the required boost falling 2.9x across the cluster where the potential-driven form falls 1.5x   (best beta = 0.376, rms 0.080 dex, worst-radius miss 1.54x; |Phi| slope -0.143 against the required -0.317. This proposal is decided by E4, not by E3)

  E4  Door 2's own falsifier.  The potential is set by the largest structure one sits in, so a galaxy INSIDE a
      cluster inherits the cluster's |Phi| and therefore its boosted a0.  The baryonic Tully-Fisher relation
      V^4 = G M a0 then shifts its zero point by F^(1/4) for cluster members relative to field galaxies.
      with the boost the clusters require, F ~ 19.4: V_flat shifts by 2.10x (0.322 dex), i.e. 1.29 dex in baryonic mass at fixed velocity
      the relation's intrinsic scatter is about 0.10 dex in mass, so this is a 13 sigma offset between cluster members and the field
  [PASS] E4 [door 2 FAILS] the boost the clusters require would displace the baryonic Tully-Fisher relation of cluster member galaxies by several dex in mass relative to field galaxies, which is excluded: the relation is observed to be the same in both environments   (predicted offset 1.29 dex in baryonic mass, about 13 times the relation's scatter; cluster and field spirals are observed to share one relation)

  E5  method.  In the accompanying symbolic script the quantities asserted are the same ones assigned:
      p_dust = 0 then assert w == 0; tilt_energy = 0 then assert tilt_energy == 0.  Such assertions cannot fail,
      so they certify nothing.  The numerical script does load the real X-COP data and fit it, and its own
      reported radial slope of -0.30 agrees with the value derived independently here.
  [PASS] E5 [method] the symbolic certification asserts quantities it has just assigned, so it cannot fail and does not support the claim of rigour; the numerical script does use real data and its reported slope is reproduced here   (reported: tautological assertions in the symbolic script; the numerical script's -0.30 slope is confirmed independently)

  what is right in the proposal, and worth keeping: Door 1 identifies a genuinely COLD component, which is exactly
  what g04a showed the cluster needs and what the condensate cannot be; and Door 2 identifies the right degeneracy-
  breaking variable, since the potential really does distinguish a cluster core from a galaxy outskirt at equal
  acceleration.  Both diagnoses are correct.  It is the two implementations that fail, and for different reasons.

  caveats: the potential is truncated at the outermost tabulated radius, which shifts |Phi| by a constant and so
  affects F's normalisation but not the slope comparison of E3; E4 uses the median required boost across radii.  total 0s

RESULT: 0 FAIL
